// @run
// @log 7
// @hint redundant type annotation: `q` is inferred as `{b: string, a: i32}` — remove it to lean on inference
// FIELD-PERMUTED NAMED ALIASES ARE ONE TYPE, ALL THE WAY DOWN. This file was
// `soundness/xfail-miscompile-permuted-struct-fields.vl` — the `xfail-miscompile-` kind's
// first and only member — and it is flipped to `@run` under that kind's own rule: when the
// compiler stops being wrong, drop the prefix and pin the right behaviour instead.
//
// The 2026-08 ruling is that `{a: i32, b: string}` and `{b: string, a: i32}` are THE SAME
// TYPE. The checker always agreed; the emitter minted two rows, gave them two wasm struct
// types, and the assignment was check-clean INVALID WASM.
//
// IT TOOK TWO COORDINATED CHANGES, and the diagnosis that found them came from printing the
// tables rather than reading the code — four readings of this area were each wrong.
// `sNames` beside `sHeapIdx` showed `[P/0, Q/1]` for the permuted pair against `[P/0, Q/0]`
// for a same-order one, which located the merge at heap-type assignment; instrumenting
// `repStructSlotsTwin` showed it never fired for the pair at all, which located the block at
// the FIRST of `buildStructTwins`' two gates:
//
//   keys[i] = slotCanonId(i)
//   if keys[j] == keys[i] && structFieldCodesEq(j, i) == 1 { rep = j }
//
//   · `repCanonId`'s `TyObj` arm walked `objFieldNames` in DECLARATION order, so the twins
//     keyed differently and were never candidates. It now SORTS the (name-sid, child-id)
//     pairs — safe by construction, not by measurement: an object's field names are unique,
//     so ordering by sid is a bijection on the field set, and sorting can merge twins but
//     can never collide two distinct types.
//   · `structFieldCodesEq` compares `sFieldTypes[sFieldStart[x] + i]` POSITIONALLY, and that
//     is a real guard — two rows may share a heap type only if their emitted field SEQUENCE
//     matches. So a named declaration is now laid out in the order of an existing row with
//     the same field-name set, when one exists.
//
// NEITHER ALONE DOES ANYTHING. Sorting the key without the layout leaves the pair failing
// the second gate; canonicalising the layout without the key leaves them never compared. An
// earlier note called the layout half "refuted" on exactly that evidence, which was the
// wrong word for necessary-but-not-sufficient.
//
// `tdFlat == 0` GUARDS THE RULING'S OWN EXCEPTION: a `flat type`'s field order IS its
// layout (`memory/flat-layout-grid.vl` — "order is layout, and nothing is reordered to pack
// better"), so a flat record is never reordered. It is one condition because the AST already
// carries the distinction.
//
// Measured across 14 positions — param, binding, return, array element, struct field, map
// value, generic argument, nullable, `.push`, closure parameter, an all-i32 permutation, and
// the three that always worked (same-order inline, permuted inline, union boxing): all 14
// now run. `types/permuted-inline-shape-shares-row.vl` is the control for the half that was
// already correct.
type P = { a: i32, b: string }

type Q = { b: string, a: i32 }

function f(x: P) {
  x.a
}

const q: Q = { b: "s", a: 7 }

print(f(q))
